WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:07.460
I'm Anubhuti Mishra.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:10.670
And today I'm going to be
talking about allocating COVID

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:15.210
19 vaccines in West Africa.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.700
So before I get
into that, I just

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.900
want to spend a few minutes
talking about my organization.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.530
I work with Palladium
Group, which

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:25.150
is an international
development organization.

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:29.580
We work with a lot of
USAID funded projects

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.500
in West Africa
and Latin America.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:37.020
I primarily work on this
big USA-funded project

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:41.400
called DataFi, where
we primarily work on HIV

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:46.430
and COVID-19 programs in
Latin America and West Africa.

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:49.550
So we do a lot of data
science in public health.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:53.600
That involves something like
this building optimization

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:58.970
models to building anomaly
detection models for data

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:02.750
quality assessment to
building risk score models

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:07.130
to figure out who should be,
who in the population

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:08.940
is at a higher risk for HIV.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:14.370
So that you can optimize
your testing resources.

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:19.950
So in 2021, we were
approached by a couple

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:24.660
of West African countries with
a very interesting problem

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.330
that there was an
influx of millions

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:32.970
of vaccines from Covax,
Gavi, World Bank, USA

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:35.500
into African countries.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.380
But suddenly they
found themselves

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:41.590
with more than enough
vaccines to meet their needs.

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:45.060
But they found
themselves very lacking

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.150
when it comes to
supply chain issues.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:51.720
Just the infrastructure
around sending vaccines

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:52.750
across the country.

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:57.060
Vaccinating millions
of adults in a period

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:01.180
of less than a year or 1 and
1/2 years was a huge problem.

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:04.200
And we found with a
little bit of research

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.120
and talking to our
country partners,

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:09.870
we found that a lot of countries
ended up wasting vaccines

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:14.280
because they were just not able
to administer those vaccines

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.770
before their expiration date.

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:18.570
So this was a very
interesting problem,

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:21.450
very different from what
we were seeing in countries

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:26.070
like USA or European countries.

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:29.170
So we thought, OK, this
is a great opportunity.

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:32.880
Let's build an allocation model,
let's build an optimization

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.380
model where we'll take into
consideration a lot of factors,

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.860
like time to
expiration or travel

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:44.080
time between distribution
centers to vaccine sites.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.570
How many human how much staff
you have available at vaccine

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:51.090
sites to hesitancy
and everything.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:54.640
We had a whole model in place.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:58.420
But then we faced reality.

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:02.600
So this is what the
vaccination sites look,

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.720
look like in country.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:08.740
This is a picture of a mobile
vaccine site from Cote d'Ivoire

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:10.420
or Ivory Coast.

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:14.470
And we found that data
was being collected

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:16.510
across different platforms.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:19.960
Some sites had
tablets, some sites

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:24.280
had paper based data entry.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:28.540
This is what vaccine
storage site looked like.

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:30.640
Some of the better
equipped sites

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.010
had a data entry
person with a tablet

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:38.100
who was able to enter patient
level data into an app.

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:41.040
So essentially we
found ourselves

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.110
in an extremely low
quality data environment.

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:48.180
So none of the pillars that
our beautiful optimization

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:50.440
models could stand on existed.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:54.570
We found that, like I said,
data was being collected

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:56.290
across different platforms.

00:03:56.290 --> 00:04:01.410
There was a lot of fragmentation
across the data collection

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:02.280
chain.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:05.040
Then we found that
the stock data

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:08.320
was being collected and
managed in an Excel based tool.

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:12.630
There were four different
types of vaccines.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:17.850
There was J&amp;amp;J and Sinopharm
and Pfizer and AstraZeneca.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:22.650
There was no data on health care
stuff available at site level.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:28.170
So we suddenly found ourselves
in a very challenging

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:31.110
environment.

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:37.680
So this is where we
decided that we slightly

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.530
decided to change the
final goal of our work.

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:45.150
So we were no longer looking at
building an exact optimization

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:52.800
model that should give you the
exactly correct data on how

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.400
to allocate vaccines.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:57.510
We figured that currently
the Ministry of Health

00:04:57.510 --> 00:05:01.290
is just using whatever
happens haphazardly,

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:03.840
using whatever information
they have at hand, most often,

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:07.980
more often than not. It
is just based on what,

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:13.020
what is the usage rate at a
vaccination site or eligible

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:13.690
population.

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:16.090
They are not looking
at any other factors.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:18.900
So we decided to use
some open source data,

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.060
pull in whatever
proprietary data

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:25.590
we could get from the government
and combine all of that

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:30.130
to create a vaccine
allocation tool.

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:33.510
So this is a list of all
sorts of open source data

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:37.560
that we pulled into our tool.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:40.650
We pulled in population
data from WorldPop.

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:44.280
There were there was
data on health care sites

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:46.180
across Sub-Saharan Africa.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:49.170
Then we were able to use
a bunch of R packages

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:52.410
to calculate travel time,
to calculate catchment area

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:55.140
for vaccine sites.

00:05:55.140 --> 00:06:00.090
And that combined with
a lot of country data.

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:05.490
So even the list of
data sets listed here

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:07.210
were not available at the start.

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:11.490
We had to and we are still
working with the country a lot

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:13.270
to get these data sets.

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:16.980
They are they started
collecting data

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:21.090
on healthcare staff
available at the site level

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:24.370
after we requested them to
share that data with us.

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:28.530
We are working with them on
how to collect these data

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:35.720
sets in a routine and a
systemic, systematic format.

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:40.790
So then this was our approach
to creating this vaccine

00:06:40.790 --> 00:06:44.660
allocation tool where we would
combine a bunch of open source

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:47.870
data with country specific data
and create this tool, which

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:50.600
gives you some sort
of an estimate for how

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:51.880
to allocate vaccines.

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:54.650
We are no longer asking
the Ministry of Health

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:57.440
to look at this
tool as an end all,

00:06:57.440 --> 00:06:59.520
be all for allocating vaccines.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:02.480
They should be looking
at their ground realities

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:06.410
and comparing with the
suggestions from this model

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:12.590
and then making their decisions
on how to allocate the tool.

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:19.040
So when it came to where to
build this tool, we wanted to

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:23.720
and several I think
all the presenters

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:25.940
before me have mentioned
that when you are working

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:30.320
in public sector, you're often
working with extremely low data

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:31.940
environment.

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:35.180
Low cost is always an
issue, so you're always

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:37.340
relying on open source tools.

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:39.980
So we wanted to
build a low cost,

00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:46.140
easy to use interactive
and portable app.

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:48.680
And then of course,
Shiny is the only thing

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:50.400
that came to all our minds.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:55.580
So we built a Shiny app and
this is what the landing

00:07:55.580 --> 00:08:00.710
page looks like. It exists in.

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:03.430
Let me start this.

00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:08.350
Yeah it exists in both
English and French right now.

00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:13.140
So the app lets you change
the input language so

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:17.550
that because most of our
country staff speaks French.

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:20.670
Cote d'Ivoire is a
French dominant country.

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:25.180
None of the nobody on the data
science teams speaks French.

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:30.470
So we have an English
version for ourselves.

00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:38.210
So this is our Shiny app
and you will see that,

00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:39.809
on some of the other tabs,

00:08:39.809 --> 00:08:42.320
we still have text in
both English and French.

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:44.310
That's for our
internal reference.

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:47.910
This app has still
not been rolled out.

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:50.700
But OK?

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:57.730
Yeah so this the second tab
called Stoock Data essentially

00:08:57.730 --> 00:09:00.160
just displays all the
stock data that we

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:04.330
processed from the
ministry's Excel based tool.

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:08.540
You can look at what types of
vaccines are available, their

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:11.800
batch number, their expiration
date, number of doses,

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:13.640
number of vials available.

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:21.225
And we use this information
to build the allocation model.

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:32.100
Then we have the
Distribution Network which

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:35.980
displays the distribution
network for Cote d'Ivoire.

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:39.510
In Cote d'Ivoire, the vaccines
come to a national distribution

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:40.010
center.

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:42.900
From there, they go to
regional distribution center,

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:47.070
which from there on, they go to
district level, a distribution

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:50.170
center, and then from there
they are sent to vaccine sites.

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:52.980
We've divided these
into separate maps

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:56.430
because obviously it was very
difficult to view everything

00:09:56.430 --> 00:09:58.620
in a single map.

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:07.260
So on the left hand side,
you can see this regional

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:10.000
to district level
distribution maps.

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:13.180
And then on the right hand
side, you can see them.

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:15.960
The connection between the
center, the national level

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:17.050
distribution center.

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:19.310
The regional
distribution center.

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:26.920
Then these are the location
of all the vaccination

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:28.620
sites across the country.

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:33.940
So in our input data, we do
have the entire distribution

00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:34.630
network.

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:38.500
So which sites are assigned
to which districts.

00:10:38.500 --> 00:10:42.010
And this is a list of
all vaccination sites.

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:47.650
And users can come in here and
they can select or unselect

00:10:47.650 --> 00:10:49.660
any vaccination sites,
because we often

00:10:49.660 --> 00:10:54.130
see that the Ministry will roll
out during they'll have periods

00:10:54.130 --> 00:11:01.030
of vaccine campaigns where
they will roll out 50 to 100

00:11:01.030 --> 00:11:03.320
additional vaccine,
mobile vaccination sites.

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:05.060
So that can be added here.

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:06.820
And then when they're
done with them,

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:09.100
they can be removed
from the model.

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:11.160
So you can see users can

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:16.600
come in and check or
uncheck, and then they

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:21.420
can save the list
of vaccine sites.

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:24.450
Once that is done, they
can go over to the Proposed

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:26.530
Distribution tab.

00:11:26.530 --> 00:11:29.280
This allows you the
first user input here

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:30.850
we have is number of days.

00:11:30.850 --> 00:11:34.050
Because of the
complexity of the model,

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:39.390
we needed to ascertain how many
days does the Ministry have

00:11:39.390 --> 00:11:42.450
to allocate these
number of vaccines.

00:11:42.450 --> 00:11:47.550
So they can decide here that
we have these current batches

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:49.320
and based on the
expiration date,

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:51.750
we want to allocate
all of these vaccines

00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:54.660
in the next 20 days or 30 days.

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:57.510
They are, the default
we've used this 30 days,

00:11:57.510 --> 00:12:00.210
based on the
ministry's feedback

00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:01.980
and they can save
this information

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:03.730
and then run the model.

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:07.440
So once all of this
information is saved,

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:11.820
we click this button
to run the model which

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:16.020
will produce your proposed
distribution maps.

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:20.280
So this takes a few seconds,
but this is essentially

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:24.860
a linear optimization
model with

00:12:24.860 --> 00:12:30.230
in with constraints
around expiration date,

00:12:30.230 --> 00:12:33.640
time to vaccinate,

00:12:33.640 --> 00:12:36.700
eligible population. We
will walk through the list

00:12:36.700 --> 00:12:38.950
of constraints

00:12:38.950 --> 00:12:39.790
in a bit.

00:12:39.790 --> 00:12:41.700
So this is the proposed.

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:45.910
This is a distribution map
that is generated by the app.

00:12:45.910 --> 00:12:50.170
You can look at
the map and then,

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:54.970
if you hover over any
of the vaccine sites,

00:12:54.970 --> 00:12:58.720
it will show you the name, the
regional depot and the district

00:12:58.720 --> 00:12:59.300
depot

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:02.480
that site is connected to,
and the number of vaccines

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:06.000
of each type allocated
to that vaccine site.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:08.680
So here we can see
that this site has

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:10.670
been allocated only Sinopharm.

00:13:10.670 --> 00:13:15.940
So 722 doses of Sinopharm
and none of the other ones.

00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:19.210
Then we can see here
the site has been

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:23.780
allocated 916 doses of Pfizer.

00:13:23.780 --> 00:13:25.230
So, so on and so forth.

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:30.890
You can click on any of the
site and look at how many doses

00:13:30.890 --> 00:13:33.540
have been allocated

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:34.925
to that particular side.

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:44.290
Then there's the map
below, where, of course,

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:46.550
the above map was a
little difficult to read.

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:53.300
So you can filter this
map on whichever district.

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:54.470
You want to look at.

00:13:54.470 --> 00:13:57.520
And here we've

00:13:57.520 --> 00:14:01.000
selected the Abobo
East, and you can

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:04.510
zoom in and look at the
number of vaccines allocated

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:08.470
to each of the vaccination
sites connected to this district

00:14:08.470 --> 00:14:09.450
distribution center.

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:20.140
So yeah, this is basically
the model once this

00:14:20.140 --> 00:14:22.220
is done,

00:14:22.220 --> 00:14:22.910
you can.

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:26.020
Users can go over
to the Results tab

00:14:26.020 --> 00:14:29.560
where they can view all of these
results in a tabular format.

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:34.300
They can see which how many
doses of each type of vaccine

00:14:34.300 --> 00:14:36.690
has been assigned to
each vaccination site.

00:14:36.690 --> 00:14:40.330
They can download these results,
share it with their team,

00:14:40.330 --> 00:14:44.670
and make decisions
based on this.

00:14:44.670 --> 00:14:48.660
Then we just have a tab
for Model Documentation.

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:51.440
But this is

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:58.170
the Shiny app for
vaccine allocation tool.

00:14:58.170 --> 00:15:00.410
We still haven't launched it.

00:15:00.410 --> 00:15:02.160
Next month we are going
to have a training

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:04.650
session with our
Ministry of Health

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:11.110
and their COVID-19
COVID-19 team in country.

00:15:11.110 --> 00:15:15.480
And once this is done, we
will be transitioning it

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:16.480
off to the team.

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:22.590
So that they can update and use
this tool for making decisions,

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:25.320
allocation decisions
going forward.

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:27.690
So like I said,
this is essentially

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:33.930
a linear optimization problem
where our main focus was

00:15:33.930 --> 00:15:36.630
our decision variable
was to figure out

00:15:36.630 --> 00:15:40.810
how many vaccines should
be allocated to each site.

00:15:40.810 --> 00:15:46.420
And the optimisation objective
was to minimize wastage

00:15:46.420 --> 00:15:49.360
and while maximizing
the number of people

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:53.950
vaccinated in the eligible
population. And we ran into,

00:15:53.950 --> 00:15:56.560
we started with a smaller
list of constraints,

00:15:56.560 --> 00:16:01.060
but over time we ran into
as you work with the data,

00:16:01.060 --> 00:16:05.340
you figured out some
additional constraints.

00:16:05.340 --> 00:16:08.360
So it included that.

00:16:08.360 --> 00:16:10.990
We had to rank
prioritize batches based

00:16:10.990 --> 00:16:13.490
on their time to expiration.

00:16:13.490 --> 00:16:17.110
So not only did we have four
different types of vaccines

00:16:17.110 --> 00:16:20.540
for each type of vaccine, you
could have multiple batches.

00:16:20.540 --> 00:16:23.680
So we had to make sure
that the model starts

00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:28.180
to allocate the batches
that expire first

00:16:28.180 --> 00:16:33.410
before it allocates batches that
have more time to expiration.

00:16:33.410 --> 00:16:38.260
Then we yeah, we had to there
were a lot of constraints

00:16:38.260 --> 00:16:39.970
around expiration
date, making sure

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:43.660
that it's delivered
and administered

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:46.540
before its expiration
date, that there

00:16:46.540 --> 00:16:51.830
are enough people to administer
those vaccines at the site.

00:16:51.830 --> 00:16:54.835
So yeah, this is the,

00:16:57.590 --> 00:17:00.420
our vaccine allocation tool.

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:02.900
There was a lot of
hesitancy from the Ministry

00:17:02.900 --> 00:17:08.060
initially about using R
and R Shiny. As a lot of you

00:17:08.060 --> 00:17:10.940
might know that normally
in public health care,

00:17:10.940 --> 00:17:12.859
especially in Africa,
countries tend

00:17:12.859 --> 00:17:15.270
to use these as two platform.

00:17:15.270 --> 00:17:18.770
So they were extremely
hesitant to work

00:17:18.770 --> 00:17:24.770
with a new platform, a
separate one for their COVID 19

00:17:24.770 --> 00:17:25.859
vaccination needs.

00:17:25.859 --> 00:17:29.090
But once they saw what we
did to bring them on board

00:17:29.090 --> 00:17:32.570
was that before we even got
any data from the country,

00:17:32.570 --> 00:17:35.750
we build a mock app, we
travel to the country,

00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:39.270
we had a training requirements
gathering workshop.

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:40.800
We showed them the app.

00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:43.340
They were able to use it
and see how useful it can

00:17:43.340 --> 00:17:45.090
be for their decision making.

00:17:45.090 --> 00:17:48.210
And that was essentially all
we needed to get them on board.

00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:51.740
And since then they
have been extremely

00:17:51.740 --> 00:17:54.680
active and instrumental in
ensuring that we receive

00:17:54.680 --> 00:17:56.760
all the data that we need.

00:17:56.760 --> 00:17:59.630
We have regular meetings
with the Ministry

00:17:59.630 --> 00:18:02.720
of Health folks and
their COVID-19 task force

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:07.400
to ensure that they are
abreast with all the changes

00:18:07.400 --> 00:18:10.980
that are happening that we
are making to the model.

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:15.420
And we are still figuring
out some of the challenges

00:18:15.420 --> 00:18:19.280
with transitioning this tool
over to the country team.

00:18:19.280 --> 00:18:24.640
Like I said, you know, they
this is a new tool for them.

00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:26.710
They don't have R
programmers on their team.

00:18:26.710 --> 00:18:32.550
So we will be continuing
to work with the Ministry

00:18:32.550 --> 00:18:36.460
to figure out those challenges.

00:18:36.460 --> 00:18:40.450
But yeah, and.

00:18:40.450 --> 00:18:42.140
That's where my talk ends.

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:46.600
I would like to advertise
this a little bit

00:18:46.600 --> 00:18:50.560
since I got a chance that I
am hiring a senior associate

00:18:50.560 --> 00:18:51.680
in data science.

00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:54.490
Ours is a small but
growing team and we work

00:18:54.490 --> 00:18:56.050
on very interesting problems.

00:18:56.050 --> 00:18:59.590
We don't have huge
data sets, but we do

00:18:59.590 --> 00:19:01.850
build very interesting models.

00:19:01.850 --> 00:19:04.970
So do get in touch with
me if you have any.

00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:08.400
If you're interested.

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:11.270
OK it's like anybody.

